00:00:01.510 --> 00:00:03.270 align:start position:0%
this morning firefighters are making  some progress in their battle to contain  the fire threat to california's giant  sequoias the washburn fire in yosemite  national park is now 22 percent  contained as firefighters work to  protect some of the largest and oldest  trees in the world so far it has burned  more than four square miles of historic  parkland yosemite is partly closed  because of the fire and hundreds of  people have been evacuated jonathan  vigliotti is there  the fire fight in yosemite rages on  time lapse video shows the thick plumes  of dark smoke that triggered air quality  alerts over the entire region reaching  the bay area and as far north as oregon  despite flames being attacked from all  angles the washburn fire continues to  expand we are getting as many resources  as we possibly can we have tankers we  have helicopters we have dozers but the  safest way to fight the fire is also the  most difficult on foot in some cases  firefighters have had to hike more than  a mile in ash like this to reach the  flames it's all part of a multi-faceted  effort to save these national treasures  the giant sequoias are known for being  fire resistant but even they have a  limit you'll look up and you'll see  200 foot flame lengths and that kind of  fire is why they are less resistant to  survival in the past six years over 85  percent of the giant sequoia habitat in  the sierra nevada has been hit by  wildfire compared to just 25 percent the  previous century while the worst of the  washburn fire is moving away from the  sequoias it's now burning in a remote  area of the park that's hard to reach  fire crews are now re-strategizing  they're starting small fires known as  back burns to try to coax the flames  down the hillside the crews will go up  to the actual uncontrolled fire's edge  and they'll start working off of that  uncontrolled edge bringing fire down the  slope  and around 500 giant sequoias are still  threatened this morning some are 3000  years old fire crews are now working to  establish a perimeter around them they  also put down a water sprinkler system  we reported on this yesterday park  rangers tell me this is the first time  they've had to use this technique  in the entire park's history  gail  jonathan thank you we all want the trees  to survive
 

